russia's invasion of ukraine has  triggered a historic mass movement of  people not seen in europe in decades  more than a million people have already  freed the country according to the un's  refugee agency and that number is  growing by the day  among those fleeing russia's bombs are  african students studying at  universities in ukraine according to  ukrainian officials there were about 16  000 of them before the conflict began  some of them have reported receiving  racist treatment from ukrainian  officials who they say pushed them back  as they tried to flee  now some african students are still in  ukraine and trying to get out and others  are in other european countries some  have left for africa like one medical  student who is now safely back home in  ghana she shared her experience with us  together again  priscilla ajar and her father samuel  after a terrifying week  the fifth year medical student was  studying at a university in western  ukraine when the war broke out  it was very tough it was horrible like  i remember i was shivering literally  when my like my school said that okay  you guys could go home i was like which  means that the school sees the intensity  of it now  there was similar fear back home in  ghana her family was closely monitoring  events in faraway ukraine and not for  the first time  the anxiety is a reminiscent of what  happened in 2014  because in 2014  my son was also in donetsk  and the same  issue happened and they had to virtually  run from the next to the capital kiev so  when the second one also started from  last week  a whole lot of  anxiety emotional  trauma let's put it that way the entire  family was put into that  priscilla made for the border closest to  her city  and we took a bus so after we just took  a bus and then we after some time the  buses were not moving vehicles are not  moving anymore so we had to trek for i  think over over an hour and a half  yes so that's how we got to the border  and then the water  it was messy  so many people ukrainians ghanaians  nigerians zimbabweans  egyptians indians everybody everybody in  chelsea is going there  she crossed into romania and like many  other ghanaian students  made it back home safely  but priscilla still has major concerns  as the war escalates in ukraine  so let's say that they destroy  everything in the country the country  has to go through redevelopmental stages  again so what is  what what like what's at stake for us i  don't know if our school buildings will  be destroyed whatever  so it's quite  burdensome and it's worrying very  worrying  that we don't know if we can go back to  school if we would finish  if we would complete are we going to go  back to square one for what the answers  might not come for a while  but being with family can be the great  comfort priscilla and others like her  need right now  now priscilla edgar and others are  safely home now but many others are not  while some have made it out of ukraine  and are now in neighboring countries  there are still some african nationals  stuck in ukraine and trying to get out  earlier i spoke to husseini uma madakawi  he's a chairperson of the nigerian  diaspora in hungary he was at the border  between hungary and ukraine  that we are here  to assist nigerians guide them  how to get to the city  we brought along with us from budapest  some humanitarian clothes  some winter clothes  and  some good items  for those who arrived from  ukraine  we help them  uh  guide them  where  the  connections  here  uh we were hearing or have been hearing  accounts of africans trying to leave  ukraine they've been reporting that  they've been not allowed into trains  leaving ukraine they say they've  experienced racism and ill treatment  from ukrainian officials that are on the  ground  are you still hearing accounts is this  still continuing to happen as people try  to leave ukraine  well uh we hear about this uh situation  but we cannot verify it because our job  is limited to uh coming to hungarian  border what's happening  at the ukrainian buddha we we have no  idea but we have people  um  calling and making complaints that they  are being taken off from the train from  the buses  but we don't know for what what reason  husseini how many people do you know of  that are still in ukraine and want to  get out what are they telling you  it's very difficult to predict how many  people  are still in ukraine because we don't  have the actual number of of them before  now  so by all i know that  it's not only me all the executives of  needle  uh hungary  we share our phone numbers they are  calling us getting uh  uh  to get information  on what to do  uh how to come from hungary  and what help can we offer that we tell  them  what we can do for them and uh some  parents of course out of uh desperation  they are calling and crying and telling  us that  their children  are  dying their children  are in the border and they don't know  what to do we try to calm them down  and tell them we are on top of the  situation  in synchron with the embassy  we are trying to do our best  to see what we can do for them how we  can help them we  guide many of them to the hotel to a  place where the charity organization  is providing shelter for them the needle  hungry now provide food for them we have  volunteers who go there to cook for them  and we should hear all the pictures of  what we  yes  sorry we're talking about the people who  are still in ukraine right now we'll  talk about those that have left  but for those who are still in ukraine  what kind of support do they need to  leave  uh many of them are on their way  so the question they asked mainly is  that  which is the easiest order for them  to cross  so as i told you earlier that as they  are scattered there in ukraine in  different cities whichever border is  closer for them to go that's where they  will advice  so we don't receive them to come into  hungary because um  some of them  are not closer to hungarian water  okay  and for those that have now managed to  get out and are not some are in poland  slovakia and others  in hungary where you are what is their  situation there  the situation is still uh  chaotic  we're trying to gather them  register them  put them in the hotel we have charity  organizations that offer  uh  including some  these three countries of our places for  those coming in  to to accommodate them for a  short period of time and we are just  directing them there  that was husseini uma madakawi speaking  to us from the border between hungary  and ukraine now  the first group of more than 400  nigerians evacuated from ukraine have  landed in abuja  the group comprised mostly of students  they arrived on a government chartered  flight from romania's capital bucharest  they are among 14 000 nigerian nationals  who were known to be in ukraine  before the war began that's according to  the nigerian foreign ministry's figures  now it is unclear if and when  they can go back  to ukraine
 

